  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Q4-2026 is the trough as guided: sulfur at 800-850/lt in COGS, phosphate operating rate near 50%. Cheaper contracted sulfur reaches COGS through 2027 and DAP prices give back part of the cost-push rise, so revenue is flat-to-down while the stripping margin rebuilds. Margin recovers 13% -> 17% -> 18.5% mid-cycle, between the 16.8% ex-supercycle and 20.7% all-in FY2018-25 averages. Araxa, Carlsbad and Taquari leaving the portfolio lets capex drift from ~1.3bn to ~1.1-1.15bn plus ~100 of ARO cash. Potash stays well supplied at ~280/t. Mature unlevered owner FCF ~650-700, in line with the FY2018-25 record.",
    "bull": "The sulfur shock leaves phosphate structurally tight: Chinese export restraint persists, US countervailing duties on Moroccan/Russian product survive litigation, and Florida/Louisiana runs back above 65% utilisation at high stripping margins. Potash firms as Belarus volumes stay logistically constrained. Mid-cycle margin 22% (the FY2018-25 average including 2021-22 is 20.7%), the pruned portfolio holds capex under 10% of sales, and Brazil distribution recovers with farmer credit.",
    "bear": "Sulfur and ammonia stay elevated on Hormuz/Kazakhstan disruption while affordability destroys phosphate demand (management already sees shipments down ~20mt), so price cannot outrun cost. New potash supply (Belarus sanction relief, greenfield tonnes) pushes MOP back toward 220-240/t; Chinese low-analysis phosphate keeps taking Brazilian share. Margin settles at 15%, below the 16.8% ex-supercycle average, with trapped losses keeping the cash tax rate high. On this path enterprise value does not cover ~5.6bn of net debt and the investment-grade rating and dividend are at risk."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Mosaic is valued on normalised mid-cycle cash flow, not on the trough it is in or the 2022 peak. The awkward fact is cash conversion: capex plus reclamation absorbs roughly half of mid-cycle EBITDA and cash taxes are heavy because Canadian potash profits cannot be sheltered by US/Brazilian losses, so ~2.2bn of mid-cycle EBITDA becomes only ~650-700 of unlevered owner FCF - which matches what the company actually produced over FY2018-25. Against ~5.6bn of net debt that leaves equity as a geared residual: small changes in the mid-cycle margin move per-share value several-fold, and the Ma'aden stake is a large share of what is left.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Mid-cycle EBITDA margin is the whole valuation: each 1pp is worth roughly US$3/share at this leverage",
      "Sustaining capex: if the pruned portfolio really runs on ~1.0bn rather than ~1.15bn + ARO, base value rises materially",
      "Ma'aden stake (1.79bn at 30-Sep-2026) is taken at quoted value with no tax, lock-up or block discount",
      "Leverage: net debt 5.6bn plus 0.4bn structured payables against TTM EBITDA of ~1.25bn; a prolonged trough forces asset sales, a dividend cut or equity",
      "ARO of 2.5bn is carried in the flows at ~100/yr above accretion; a regulatory step-up in gypstack closure spending is not modelled",
      "The 14x terminal cap binds in every scenario; at 12x base value falls by roughly US$1.5-2/share"
    ]
  },
